          "session_id": "full-g-md-022",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been on a proton pump inhibitor for six years. Is long-term use a concern, what does guidance describe about review, and how is reflux coded?",
          "answer": "You should regularly review your proton pump inhibitor (PPI) treatment with your healthcare provider, especially since you've been on the medication for an extended period. Long-term PPI use can lead to potential complications, including lower vitamin B12 levels, so periodic measurement of vitamin levels is recommended (source: Gastroenterology society clinical guideline on GERD; guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy).\n\nAnnual reviews are advisable once your treatment regimen stabilizes. These reviews are important for assessing the ongoing need for the PPI and monitoring for any changes in symptoms that might require further evaluation (source: guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features). If you experience any significant changes in symptoms, an earlier review is suggested (source: research specialist).\n\nGastroesophageal reflux is classified as gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) if it occurs frequently enough or causes troubling symptoms or esophageal damage (source: General clinical reference on GERD). For coding purposes, GERD is classified in ICD-10 under codes starting with K21 (source: coding-gerd-k21). If you have concerns regarding your PPI usage or reflux symptoms, consider discussing them with your healthcare provider.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
